<Doc
  title="Privacy | Sub20"
  description="Sub20 sends nothing about you anywhere. Your videos stay on your computer, and the one request it makes by itself is the update check."
  heading="Privacy at Sub20"
  lead="Sub20 sends nothing about you anywhere. Your videos never leave your computer, there is no account, and no part of it counts or tracks you."
>
  <h2>The desktop app</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Your videos</b> are read and written on your computer only. Nothing is uploaded.</li>
    <li><b>No telemetry.</b> Sub20 doesn't report how you use it, what you compress, or when it crashes.</li>
    <li><b>The update check</b> is the only request it makes by itself. When Sub20 opens, it reads one file from GitHub, <code>latest.json</code> on the latest release, to see whether there is a newer version. Settings, Updates turns it off.</li>
    <li><b>Download FFmpeg</b>, offered by Sub20 without FFmpeg when your computer has none, fetches FFmpeg from Sub20's own release on GitHub, only when you click it.</li>
    <li><b>Copy diagnostics</b> (Settings, About) puts a report on your clipboard for a bug report: versions, your system, the graphics encoders found, and the last job's FFmpeg log, with your home folder written as <code>~</code>. It goes nowhere unless you paste it.</li>
    <li><b>The crash log</b> stays in the app's log folder on your computer.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>GitHub serves the update file and the downloads, so it sees those requests as it sees any visit. Its <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy statement</a> says what it keeps.</p>

  <h2>Sub20 Lite</h2>
  <p>Lite runs in your browser. It reads your videos and encodes them on your own device; the video never leaves it. After the first visit it works without a connection.</p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No analytics, no cookies, no tracking scripts, no fonts or scripts from other sites.</li>
    <li>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub may log visitors' IP addresses for security, as its privacy statement explains.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>The code is open, so all of the above can be checked. Ask anything in an <a href={`${GITHUB}/issues`}>issue on GitHub</a>.</p>
</Doc>
